# Incremental Refactoring

## Context
- When modifying existing functionality
- When refactoring complex systems
- When updating core game mechanics
- When improving performance
- When fixing bugs in critical components

## Requirements
- Break down large refactoring tasks into smaller, manageable changes
- Only modify one component or subsystem at a time
- Maintain existing functionality while making incremental improvements
- Test each change before moving to the next modification
- Keep original code functional until the new implementation is proven
- Document each incremental change and its impact
- Preserve type safety throughout the refactoring process

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// Good: Incremental refactor of audio system
// Step 1: Add new functionality alongside existing
class AudioManager {
  private sounds: Map<string, Phaser.Sound.BaseSound>;
  private newSoundSystem: NewAudioSystem | null = null;

  // Existing method remains unchanged
  playSound(key: string): void {
    if (this.newSoundSystem?.isEnabled) {
      this.newSoundSystem.playSound(key);
    } else {
      // Original implementation remains
      const sound = this.sounds.get(key);
      if (sound) sound.play();
    }
  }
}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// Bad: Complete rewrite of audio system
// Removes all existing functionality at once
class AudioManager {
  private newSoundSystem: NewAudioSystem;

  playSound(key: string): void {
    // Old implementation completely removed
    // No fallback if new system fails
    this.newSoundSystem.playSound(key);
  }
}
```
</example>

## Critical Considerations
- Each change should be small enough to be easily reviewed and tested
- Maintain backward compatibility during transitions
- Keep a clear path to roll back changes if needed
- Ensure type safety is maintained at each step
- Consider the impact on dependent systems
- Test both old and new functionality during transition
- Document any breaking changes or deprecations 